window.NEO_FIELDKIT={"unit":"transformations-similarity-and-scale","expedition":"The Animation Studio","tools":[{"title":"Line card","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"10\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#f4f1e6\" stroke=\"#4a5a66\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M30 10V90\" stroke=\"#b0412f\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M10 64H90\" stroke=\"#2f6db0\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"36\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#b0412f\">x = 1</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"58\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2f6db0\">y = 2</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>On the line <i>x</i> = 1, every point has <i>x</i>-coordinate 1.</b> It is a vertical line.</p><p><b>On the line <i>y</i> = 2, every point has <i>y</i>-coordinate 2.</b> It is a horizontal line.</p>","room":"The Storyboard Wall","lesson":"Lessons 1 and 4 · Reflection, and what transformations keep"},{"title":"Turning card","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"36\" fill=\"#f4f1e6\" stroke=\"#4a5a66\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M50 50V20\" stroke=\"#2f3a44\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M58 18 A33 33 0 0 1 82 42\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b0412f\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M82 42l-9-2 7-6z\" fill=\"#b0412f\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"78\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f3a44\">clockwise</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>Clockwise</b> is the way a clock's hands turn. <b>Anticlockwise</b> is the other way.</p><p>A quarter turn is 90°, a half turn is 180°, and a three-quarter turn is 270°.</p>","room":"The Turntable","lesson":"Lesson 2 · Rotation"},{"title":"Grid-move card","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"10\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#f4f1e6\" stroke=\"#4a5a66\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"36\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#2f6db0\"/><path d=\"M26 36H70\" stroke=\"#3f8f4f\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M70 36V70\" stroke=\"#b0412f\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"70\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#2f6db0\"/></svg>","body":"<p><b>Right and left change the first coordinate. Up and down change the second.</b></p><p>From (2, 1), move 3 right and 4 up: (2 + 3, 1 + 4) = (5, 5).</p>","room":"The Slide Track","lesson":"Lesson 3 · Translation and column vectors"},{"title":"Same-shape card","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><path d=\"M14 70H44V50Z\" fill=\"rgba(176,65,47,.3)\" stroke=\"#2f3a44\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M86 70H56V50Z\" fill=\"rgba(47,109,176,.3)\" stroke=\"#2f3a44\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M50 20V86\" stroke=\"#4a5a66\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/></svg>","body":"<p><b>Congruent</b> shapes are exactly the same shape and size. A shape that is turned or flipped over is still congruent.</p><p>If every length is multiplied by the same number, the shape is <b>similar</b>: the same shape, a different size.</p>","room":"The Mirror Booth","lesson":"Lessons 4 and 7 · Congruence, and similar shapes"},{"title":"Scale-factor card","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"34\" fill=\"#2f3a44\"/><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"22\" fill=\"#6f8fa8\"/><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#cfe0ec\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f3a44\">× ½</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>A scale factor multiplies every length.</b></p><p>Scale factor 2: 5 cm becomes 10 cm. Scale factor <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;vertical-align:middle;text-align:center;line-height:1.1\"><span style=\"border-bottom:2px solid currentColor;padding:0 3px\">1</span><span style=\"padding:0 3px\">2</span></span>: 5 cm becomes 2.5 cm.</p>","room":"The Zoom Lens","lesson":"Lessons 6 and 8 · Enlargement, and missing lengths"},{"title":"Plan-scale card","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"12\" y=\"34\" width=\"76\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#f4f1e6\" stroke=\"#4a5a66\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M12 64v-10M31 64v-6M50 64v-10M69 64v-6M88 64v-10\" stroke=\"#2f3a44\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f3a44\">1 cm : 2 m</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>“1 cm represents 3 m” means each centimetre on the plan stands for 3 m in real life.</b></p><p>A 2.5 cm line on the plan: 2.5 × 3 = 7.5 m in real life.</p>","room":"The Set Model","lesson":"Lesson 9 · Maps and scale drawings"}]};
